					<span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/sirpudding" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(348648); return false;"  ><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=348648&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1609141588" alt="sirpudding" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=348648)"/></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/sirpudding" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(348648); return false;" >sirpudding</a></span> <span class="odate time_1520809798 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">11 Mar 2018 23:09</span>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-3735771">
									

<p>It comes, I believe from <a href="/scp-257-arc">SCP-257-ARC</a>. It was intended, I think, to be a shiboleth, and is mostly used in this way where it shows up again.</p>
<p>There's no one real world definition of &quot;black moon&quot; and it isn't an established astronomical term-of-art. Depending on the source, black moons can be:</p>
<ul>
<li>The second new moon in the same month.</li>
<li>The third new moon in a season of four new moons.</li>
<li>No new moon in February.</li>
<li>No full moon in February.</li>
</ul>
<p>The moon, as it interacts with the Earth's magnetic field, with solar radiation and cosmic radiation can produce or reflect many different electromagnetic signals, and can be said to be howling at times.</p>

							</div>

								
					
